            </a>
            <a href="/how-to-use-detergent-in-a-pressure-washer/" class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow no-underline group">
              <p class="text-[#16A34A] font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Detergent</p>
              <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-bold text-lg group-hover:text-[#1E90FF] transition-colors">Using Detergent Properly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-2">Why the black nozzle is the only one that draws soap.</p>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Vinyl siding wants 1,500 to 2,000 PSI with a 40 or 25 degree nozzle, which puts it comfortably inside the range of any decent electric machine. Start wide, start far back, keep the wand moving in overlapping passes, and step down one nozzle angle only if the wide tip genuinely will not shift the dirt.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The rule that matters more than any setting is the angle. Lap siding overlaps downward to shed rain, so an upward stream drives water behind the boards into the wall, where it soaks sheathing and insulation with nowhere to dry. It looks fine on the day and appears months later as damp or rot, which is why so many people make this mistake twice.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Let chemistry do the work. Pre-wet the wall and the plants, apply a house wash bottom to top through the black soap nozzle, let it dwell for several minutes, then rinse top to bottom. And reach the upper courses with a telescoping wand rather than a ladder, because a pressure washer's recoil is exactly the force that puts people on the ground.
          </p>
        </div>


